<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="UTF-8"/><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width"/><meta name="description" content="The 1983 Koga-Miyata Gentsracer-Aero GR-A: a silver-green Dutch-Japanese steel bicycle with Shimano 600 AX and Dyna-Drive."/><title>Koga-Miyata Gentsracer-Aero · The Evolution of Bikes</title><link rel="stylesheet" href={`${base}/styles/global.css`}/></head><body><EnglishHeader current="bikes" danishPath="/cykler/koga-miyata-gentsracer-aero/"/><main class="koga-page">
<header class="koga-hero"><div><p class="section-label">Previously in the collection · 1983 model</p><h1>Koga-Miyata<br/><em>Gentsracer-Aero</em></h1></div><p>A Dutch-Japanese steel bicycle built around Shimano’s brief attempt to make almost every component more aerodynamic.</p></header>
<figure class="koga-hero-image"><img src={`${imageRoot}/hero-right-side.jpg`} alt="Silver-green Koga-Miyata Gentsracer-Aero from the drive side"/><figcaption>The bicycle is no longer in the collection.</figcaption></figure>
<section class="atala-facts" aria-label="Key facts"><div><strong>1983</strong><span>Probable model year</span></div><div><strong>54 cm</strong><span>Centre-to-top size</span></div><div><strong>G-61</strong><span>Silver-green metallic</span></div><div><strong>c. 10 kg</strong><span>Catalogue weight</span></div></section>
<section class="atala-opening koga-opening"><p class="section-label">Everything had to pass through the air</p><p>The gear levers sit together on top of the down tube. Brakes, hubs, pedals and chainset follow the same idea. Whether it transformed an ordinary Sunday ride is debatable; Shimano had certainly been thorough.</p></section>
<section class="atala-story koga-dating"><div><p class="section-label">The catalogue corrects the date</p><h2>1983 — perhaps purchased in 1984</h2></div><div><p>The bicycle was previously described as a 1984 model. Koga-Miyata’s catalogues suggest otherwise.</p><p>The 1983 catalogue lists the <strong>Gentsracer-Aero GR-A</strong> in G-61 silver-green metallic. Its photograph, graphics, frame materials and Shimano 600 AX equipment match this bicycle. In 1984 the Aero name had disappeared; the Gentsracer GR had different paint and Shimano New 600 EX.</p><p>The safest description is a 1983 model, though it may have been sold or first used in 1984. Without the frame number, the date cannot be narrowed further.</p></div></section>
<section class="koga-detail-grid"><figure><img src={`${imageRoot}/model-shifters.jpg`} alt="Model name, Tange transfer and paired gear levers" loading="lazy"/><figcaption>The lever position formed part of the Aero idea.</figcaption></figure><figure><img src={`${imageRoot}/left-side.jpg`} alt="Koga-Miyata from the left" loading="lazy"/><figcaption>Minor chips and signs of use remain.</figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="atala-components koga-frame"><div><p class="section-label">Dutch name, Japanese frame</p><h2>Tange and Miyata beneath the paint</h2></div><div><p>Andries Gaastra founded Koga in 1974 after working at Batavus. The name combined the opening letters of his wife Marion Kowallik’s surname and his own.</p><p>Koga handled development, specification and assembly in the Netherlands; Miyata made the frames in Japan. This bicycle uses double-butted Tange Champion No. 2 chromoly main tubes. The catalogue identifies the stays and fork as Miyata Hi-Manga special steel.</p><p>Double-butted tubes are thicker at the joints and thinner in the middle, saving weight where equal wall thickness is unnecessary.</p></div></section>
<figure class="koga-wide-mechanics"><img src={`${imageRoot}/drivetrain.jpg`} alt="Shimano 600 AX drivetrain" loading="lazy"/><figcaption>600 AX from derailleur to chainset, with smoother shapes and enclosed parts.</figcaption></figure>
<section class="atala-story koga-ax"><div><p class="section-label">Shimano 600 AX</p><h2>Aerodynamics down to the pedal</h2></div><div><p>Shimano introduced 600 AX around 1980–81 beneath Dura-Ace AX in its Aerodynamic System Components programme.</p><p>The derailleur spring hides behind a round cover and the brakes have a low profile. Dyna-Drive’s large pedal thread allowed the platform to sit around the axle rather than above it, lowering the foot and frontal area.</p><p>The system required dedicated cranks and pedals and did not become a lasting standard. It captures Shimano’s willingness to alter familiar parts in pursuit of one coherent idea.</p></div></section>
<section class="atala-details koga-details"><figure><img src={`${imageRoot}/dyna-drive.jpg`} alt="600 AX Dyna-Drive chainset and pedal" loading="lazy"/><figcaption>The platform sat low around the large axle.</figcaption></figure><figure><img src={`${imageRoot}/rear-derailleur.jpg`} alt="600 AX rear derailleur" loading="lazy"/><figcaption>The round spring housing defines its profile.</figcaption></figure><figure><img src={`${imageRoot}/front-brake.jpg`} alt="600 AX front brake" loading="lazy"/><figcaption>The spokes show surface rust.</figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="koga-specs"><div><p class="section-label">What photographs and catalogue show</p><h2>Brief specification</h2></div><dl><div><dt>Model</dt><dd>Gentsracer-Aero GR-A</dd></div><div><dt>Frame</dt><dd>Tange Champion No. 2 / Miyata Hi-Manga</dd></div><div><dt>Groupset</dt><dd>Predominantly Shimano 600 AX</dd></div><div><dt>Chainset</dt><dd>600 AX Dyna-Drive, catalogued 42/52</dd></div><div><dt>Hubs</dt><dd>Shimano 600 AX</dd></div><div><dt>Rims</dt><dd>Mavic, exact model unconfirmed</dd></div><div><dt>Tyres shown</dt><dd>Michelin Hi-Lite Road 700 × 20C</dd></div><div><dt>Status</dt><dd>Previously in the collection</dd></div></dl></section>
<section class="atala-era koga-era"><p class="section-label">Close to the catalogue — but not untouched</p><blockquote>A coherent AX bicycle with <em>later consumables.</em></blockquote><div><p>The catalogue specifies Mavic rims, 32 spokes, a 42/52 chainset and six rear sprockets. The rim model cannot be read, while teeth should be counted before being described as certainly original.</p><p>The Michelin tyres are probably later replacements, and the saddle appears newer than the catalogued model. The bicycle is notably period-correct, but should not be called entirely catalogue-original.</p></div></section>
<section class="period-sources koga-sources"><p class="section-label">Sources</p><h2>The catalogue as a check</h2><ul><li>Koga-Miyata Dutch catalogues, 1983 and 1984.</li><li>Koga’s official company history.</li><li>Shimano, 100 Products History: Shimano 600 and 600 AX.</li></ul></section>
<section class="atala-closing koga-closing"><p>The Gentsracer-Aero records a brief period when aerodynamics meant more than a new frame shape. Even the pedal thread had to move.</p><a href={`${base}/en/bikes/`}>← Back to the bicycles</a></section></main><EnglishFooter/></body></html>
